<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bureau of Military Affairs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bureau of Military Affairs was one of the three central organs of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretariat-chancellery">Secretariat-Chancellery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finance-commission">Finance Commission</a>. It was headed by civilian officials and oversaw military administration. The overlapping jurisdictions of these councils were designed to prevent any single official from accumulating excessive power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The bureau, in Chinese the Shumiyuan or Privy Court, was headed by a Shumishi and ran a state's military forces under the Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and the Yuan. It began under Emperor Daizong of Tang (r. 762–779) as an office for provisional palace secretarial work, staffed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>. Its posts were not regularised, yet the secretaries wielded great power, influencing the succession to the throne and the appointment and dismissal of chancellors; the two Shumishi and the commanders of the Shence Army were known together as the four nobles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the Later Liang dynasty (907–923) an Office for the Veneration of Governance, the Chongzhengyuan, was founded so that state officials could administer military matters, and the Later Tang dynasty (923–936) renamed it Shumiyuan, its head assisting the Left Grand Chancellor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> raised it to stand beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-secretariat">Zhongshu Sheng</a>, which handled civil matters. The two were called the two offices, the Zhongshu Sheng being the Western Office and the Shumiyuan the Eastern.</p><p>The commissioner had a deputy, the shumi fushi, as well as notaries and vice notaries. The bureau was divided into six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> matching the Six Ministries of the Shangshu Sheng, each headed by a Recipient of Edicts; by the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> there were more than 20, later cut back to the six and one for miscellaneous matters. Late in the Northern Song many officials held the title of Shumishi as an honorific, often the Grand Chancellor at the same time, and under the Southern Song the deputy also took part in government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Other states took up the office. In the Khitan Liao dynasty it handled only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> military units, as the Southern or Chinese Bureau, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shizong-of-liao">Emperor Shizong of Liao</a> (r. 947–950) founded a Shumiyuan for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> with a northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> for military and a southern one for civil affairs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchen-jin-dynasty">Jurchen Jin dynasty</a> founded a Shumiyuan in 1123. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> the post of Shumishi was held by the heir apparent but in practice left vacant, a deputy running the bureau. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, abolished the name in 1361, during his uprising, and replaced it with the Chief Military Command.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bureau of Military Affairs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Central government was divided between three bodies, the Secretariat-Chancellery for civil administration, the <b>Bureau of Military Affairs</b> and the Finance Commission.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Jingzong built a Chinese-style government for a Tangut state: a Secretariat, a <b>Bureau of Military Affairs</b>, a Censorate and sixteen bureaus under a chancellor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Kublai's administration was centred on a Central Secretariat with six ministries beneath it, a <b>Bureau of Military Affairs</b> and a Censorate, with branch secretariats governing the provinces, the origin of China's modern provincial system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…the 10th-century generals who had made and unmade dynasties, the Song placed the army under civil officials: civilians headed the <b>Bureau of Military Affairs</b> and commanded on the front line, commanders were moved after each campaign, and successful generals were distrusted, as Yue Fei, executed in 1142, discovered.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li><li><blockquote>The Yuan kept the institutions of a Chinese state, a Central Secretariat, a <b>Bureau of Military Affairs</b> and a Censorate, but filled the posts by origin.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
